ust that whole philosophy and i think  these types of wooden coasters have the  same kind of philosophy  i don't know i just like it a lot it  also leads to roller coasters that have  no  sense of downtime if you will i  definitely know some bigger roller  coasters that at least have some  sections where you feel like  you know you're out of the action for a  bit or you're just moving in between  elements for me personally and i guess  this is kind of a hot take but for me  personally  expedition geforce kind of feels like  this i know most  roller coaster enthusiasts definitely  theme park review  still build expedition geforce as the  best roller coaster in the world or at  least one of the best in the world  but to me honestly those moments of  downtime where it just has a small  straight section or something where you  don't quite have the same forces  kind of detract from the experience and  i find that some of these smaller  courses just keep up the insanity from  beginning to end which i just love when  rollercoasters do that so yeah  i try to make that work a bit with this  coaster as well  now i'm also going on about the roller  coaster itself because  really i was mostly inspired for the  layout of this coaster everything else  i'm doing in this video is just  the necessary theming and scenery that i  need to do to make it  make it past as a realistic ride but  honestly  the focus here was really on the coaster  so i'm keeping everything quite  minimalistic  the station for instance is just a  simple  wooden shed quite generic um kind of  what i  imagine a wally be vaguely  american themed ride would be it's not  you know really quite anything it  doesn't specifically have an in-depth  story  or theming but just the vague aesthetics  of a sort of northern north american  mining town or logging town or something  like that  now just for realism's sake i am adding  a small  transfer table as well which honestly i  was a little bit stressed about for  most of me building this ride because i  really couldn't figure out how to make  this thing work eventually i'm not sure  if my  final solution was featured on the video  or not because  i start out with just building it from  that curve but i quickly realized that  it was impossible to bring the car  back from there so in the end i just had  to resort to building a small switch  track here  at the end of the station actually where  the cars could move backwards  into the transfer table because really  in this super  limited space that i moved myself into  here  it seems like the only realistic option  i also think i actually made the trains  one car  shorter because i didn't have space for  a larger transfer table so i just had to  you know base the the length of the  train on that as well  but in the end i think it works out and  it at least makes the coaster feel like  it could be real  another thing that i wanted to do for  this coaster is  obviously some custom supporting but  because this is a side project  and i don't want to get too much into it  and i don't want to do another  usury gardens where i just build a giant  custom supported wooden coaster  i'm just keeping it very minimal this  time around and trying to focus on just  the  bare basics i did  look a little bit into what gravity  group coaster supports tend to look like  because every wooden coaster i've tried  to support in planet coasters so far  was a great coasters international  coaster which  uses different support structures  i don't know exactly what their  different methods  are but it's pretty clear from looking  at their coasters that they have  different designs for the track and  supports  so for this one i mostly looked at  timber uh to try and get an idea of what  to do with the supports  and to try and also see how i can  retrofit the already existing supports  in the game  uh to my benefit instead of just  building completely new supports from  scratch because  i mean i think also these outside  supports  do give the biggest bang for the buck it  doesn't take that much effort  to add some supports onto the already  existing in-game supports  um but it just makes it look a little  bit more realistic not to mention that  the game  does tend to make under-supported wooden  coasters  you know not even for a specific company  um  style i think it's important to add  these extra wooden beams because it just  looks very flimsy if you don't  and the in-game support generation can  very  often create under-supported wooden  coasters so  i i don't want to feel like the coaster  is is  barely hanging on by a thread so i'm at  least adding some custom supports to  also make it feel a little bit more  sturdy and especially i think these  lateral supports on the outside of  curves  are quite iconic for these kinds of  smaller wooden coasters even  so i think for the the general aesthetic  of the coaster it's also quite important  to add  the biggest addition to the supports i  would say is actually  on the outside here of this curve where  i'm just hooking up to the height  of the already existing supports and  adding some lateral beams to that as  well  which honestly i think there are many  ways that you can just save time for  this like  for instance building one basic supports  that you can move around  because the whole structure is the same  height and then just connecting the  beams in between that there's  often no reason to build every single  beam individually  of course you could do that if you want  to have a very specific  um like detailed support structure as  you would see in real life but i think  this is a good  compromise where it doesn't take too  much time it might not be  exactly how it would look in real life  but it's close enough  and reasonable enough that you don't  spend too much time working on it  i've seen many people um well i say many  people but i've seen some people  really only rarely build completely  you know individually placed hand-built  wooden supports for coasters in planet  coaster  and while i have to say it looks amazing  and  i really respect the work in it it's  it's honestly something that i don't  really have the time and patience to do  and i think you can very well with with  copy paste techniques and trying to add  to what the supports in the game already  offer you can you can do  things that look almost as good in  much less time so especially because  wooden coasters are one of the most time  consuming things to build with all of  the support work included  it's it's something that i feel is is a  good way to also  prevent yourself from being deterred  from ever  trying a wooden coaster because it can  just seem like such a  daunting task if you want to do it and  you really want to do it realistically  i mean it is interesting though uh just  to get into some really  random rollercoaster trivia that in real  life  i think wooden coasters are also  insanely  complicated and take a lot of  maintenance and work  compared to steel coasters for instance  they are cheaper to buy but mostly  because the materials are cheaper  but after that they have to be  constantly maintained and they  constantly have to have their places  their tracks repaired or uh replaced  so honestly these things are very  complicated beasts and  every single plank and beam has to be in  the right place  and has to be checked regularly so  these things honestly are super  complicated  and i know i'm a rollercoaster  enthusiast  in a sense and i'll write rollercoasters  because i love the thrill of it but i  also  really respect the engineering of it and  especially with wooden coasters  the sheer amount of work that goes into  well  making it all work is really absurd and  just playing a video game and making  roller coasters in that  is really the easiest part that you can  imagine and the real life situation is  just so much more  insanely complex but yeah  i'm uh i'm gonna go back to the world of  