sters are  particularly well known for this  uh many of their their break sections  are actually  banked so that the um well i don't know  the exact  technical terms but the way that the the  brakes work the the fin  on the coaster car that has to catch  into the brakes  uh and and has to be uh slowing the  train down by friction  that has to um i mean i say friction but  usually magnetic brakes but yeah it has  to be  caught right by the brakes so because of  that reason often the brakes are  slightly banked just so that the  magnetic brakes will work  i'm not sure if the same is true for  friction brakes so i shouldn't say  anything  about it but pointing cases on a wooden  coaster you're almost always at least  going down or  up in some way and um very often not  even  entirely straight even if you think you  are so all of that just to say that  my final break run and transfer tables  also on a very slight tilt  now something that i wanted to try with  this coaster because it is quite generic  and i at least wanted to add some  scenery to it not to mention  i wanted to make this coaster blueprint  friendly as well so  um i figured this would be a good way to  do that one thing i wanted to do is add  some very generic  planters around it to kind of give it  some landscaping without having to rely  on terraforming which of course doesn't  translate with a blueprint  and my idea for that was just to build  some large planters that  kind of follow the smooth flowing  layouts of the the coaster itself  which was honestly slightly annoying to  place  um but for those who are watching this  and have never  heard or seen my trick for this usually  what i do  is i get one of those half walls where  the top is a is a half wall section and  the bottom is a full wall  i think they're meant for large windows  but honestly i'm not entirely sure what  those pieces are for  but if you sink it into the ground so  that only the half wall section is  above the ground what you get is a piece  that when you rotate it  it'll always be completely flush with  itself  like it'll rotate in a way that you  don't have to  remove replace it in any way or move it  around in a 3d space to try and  connect the next wall to the last one  all you have to do is rotate it in the  direction that you want to go  shift it out of the wall that you're  currently in and it'll automatically be  flush  with the wall that you built before it  and it just makes it way easier to build  these kind of round  shapes you never have to try and fiddle  with the pieces to make them connect  right and it just kind of makes them fun  almost  of course it's still kind of a grueling  task because you have to place many  different small wall pieces to create  these curves  and you have to try and get the curves  to to be as smooth as possible and of  course  fill it in with some mulch as well like  i'm doing here  but at the end of the day it's it's a  much easier way to do these kinds of  things  and it's surprisingly fun i think to  build these curved  wall sections especially because i feel  it looks so  out of the game because the game doesn't  really offer  any any pieces like this that are so  round out of the box  and um yeah it just makes everything uh  look a bit more smooth and  they're always great to to make  especially modern themed builds  uh come together landscaping wise  now i'm not entirely sure why i decided  to build  this whole section on a concrete pad but  i figured it would be good to have a  small  backstage section where i'm storing all  kinds of roller coaster goodies  and where um the stuff will be working  so that's really all that's that's out  here a little bit of machinery for the  transfer table some  leftover crates and pellets and storage  stuff  nothing too special also a door so that  you can actually get  into the lower section of the transfer  table there  which maybe shouldn't even have had a  floor to begin with  but i mean i'm happy keeping it this way  i just wanted to add some very small  details that also  breed the sign of life into the game and  make it look like this thing is actually  functional  and finally it was time to also  put some foliage into these planters i  didn't want to go  too crazy on the collars in here because  honestly i'm really bad  when it comes to colors and flowers and  stuff you might have noticed i don't do  a lot of flowers on my videos and  i've never really done that because i'm  not the greatest at making  good flower arrangements so i'm just  keeping everything very simple here  focusing  mostly on green stuff and a few flowers  and some colored accents  not to mention that this does seem like  a coaster that would be good for a small  lower budget  park which realistically is where these  kinds of coasters are built in real life  and that part could honestly not really  afford giant flower beds anyway so  i think it works like this and finally  i'm really just adding a bunch of  context around the right here to make it  feel like it's  actually in a map and not just in the  middle of nowhere of an empty sandbox  so most of the rest of this video will  just be spent  adding some forestry around this whole  area  there's just one more planter that you  know when i looked  and and zoomed out of the map to try and  see how everything looked  i felt like something was missing and it  just felt like  there had to be something built here on  this side to  to counterbalance all of the other  planter shapes  and i figured i might as well add this  final section here  now i want to just fast forward for a  second and move to when i finish that  planter to discuss a little bit of  pathworks  um because kind of like the supports of  the wooden coaster i want to keep the  paths  as in game and simple as possible while  at least adding some touches to it to  make sure it doesn't look too generic  so i'm leaving the in-game curbs intact  i know  some people like to just completely  replace the curbs but  honestly to me that also sounds like a  lot of work for only a minor change  and i think especially for a small build  like this it works to just try and see  if you can retro fit the curves in any  way  so for the side there i added some small  rope fences  and for these parts i'm once again doing  the half wall trick  except just with a different texture  this time around to try and fill in the  connections between  the planters also something i think  that's worth pointing out is that the  planters do have lights  at least on the sections where we don't  have these lanterns next to it  uh just those small wall lights that  shine on the path  which honestly are some of my favorite  types of lights to add to any part it's  so simple you just  sync the lights into the walls on the  sides and it just creates a really  beautiful effect  at night and you don't have to worry  about the lighting otherwise it's very  minimalistic  it's something you don't have to think  about too much but it works out really  beautifully especially with these kinds  of  minimalistic modern architecture builds  i know there isn't a lot of architecture  in this but i  imagine that these kinds of round  concrete planters could work very well  with  contemporary architecture if you're ever  building you know that kind of theme  park  but yeah the rest of this video is  really all just  the boring building tree lines which  i've done many times before  it's funny how to me at this point it  kind of feels like  i'm going back to the days of planet  coaster college  because this is what i would always use  to do of uh with the layouts of the  coasters once i was finished  just build a giant tree line around the  map that  isn't necessarily realistic because this  is of course not how a real forest would  look  but it's the kind of thing that i think  from a distance looks  realistic quite quickly without too much  effort  by stacking all of these trees in  multiple layers  but within a very tight space you can  very quickly make the outside world  invisible  while making it seem like there's  actually a very dense and complex forest  when in reality it's released a wall of  trees that quickly blocks the outside  view  so nothing too realistic there honestly  but yeah i'm gonna keep a few secrets of  this build for the final cinematics as  well so  i'm gonna skip forward and let you guys  just  enjoy some cinematic shots and the pov  of this coaster  hope you guys enjoyed this random little  planet coaster video  i'll be working on perhaps some planet  coaster stuff in the future as well i  definitely don't want to leave this game  behind i know many people are  um thinking about you know whether i'm  really coming back to this game because  honestly the problem is that there are  many games on my mind but planet coaster  will always be the game that  made my channel what it is today i  suppose and  ultimately it's also where my heart lies  with theme parks and everything  so who knows i have no idea when  or what my next planet coaster venture  is going to be  but ultimately there will probably be  some times when i feel like just making  a small side project roller coaster  anyway i'll leave you guys thanks for  watching  and we'll see each other in the next  video i guess  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  you


